@{
    Layout = "~/Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}

<section class="py-4">
    <div class="container">
        <h1 class="mb-3">Fine-tuning and Conditioning</h1>
        <p class="lead">Extend model behavior through dataset conditioning or managed fine-tunes. Understand the differences and decide when each approach is appropriate.</p>

        <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4">Prompt Conditioning</h2>
                <p class="mb-2">Conditioning uses carefully designed prompts and reference material to steer the base model without retraining.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Works with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).</li>
                    <li>Fast to iterate and low cost.</li>
                    <li>Best for content that changes frequently.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4">Fine-tuning</h2>
                <p class="mb-2">Fine-tuning adapts a model on curated examples and evaluations.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Produces a specialized model checkpoint.</li>
                    <li>Useful when prompt engineering alone cannot enforce style or domain rules.</li>
                    <li>Requires high-quality labeled data and monitoring.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card shadow-sm border-0">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4">Choosing the Right Approach</h2>
                <table class="table table-bordered mb-0">
                    <thead class="table-light">
                        <tr>
                            <th>Scenario</th>
                            <th>Prefer Conditioning when…</th>
                            <th>Prefer Fine-tuning when…</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Rapidly changing knowledge</td>
                            <td>You can retrieve latest content.</td>
                            <td>Data is stable and curated.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Strict tone or style</td>
                            <td>You can describe it in prompts.</td>
                            <td>Style has many edge cases or implicit rules.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Latency / cost</td>
                            <td>Need quick deployment and low cost.</td>
                            <td>You can afford training and evaluation cycles.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
